<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Malla dynasty (Nepal)</h1><p class="st-meta">1201 CE – 1482 CE (approx.) · capital Bhaktapur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dynasty that ruled the Kathmandu Valley from the 13th century, shaping its Newar culture before dividing into the three Malla kingdoms.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Malla period in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> begins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ari-malla">Ari Malla</a>, who took the throne in 1201. The early centuries were marked by raids from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khasa-malla-kingdom">Khasa Malla kingdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karnat-dynasty">Karnat dynasty</a>, and by rivalries between noble houses based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur-nepal">Patan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasthiti-malla">Jayasthiti Malla</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> in the late 14th century, is remembered for codifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and social rules and patronising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Newari learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaksha-malla">Yaksha Malla</a> extended the kingdom and built temples in Bhaktapur. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newar</a> artisans produced metalwork, painting and architecture for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist patrons, and the valley profited from trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaksha-malla">Yaksha Malla's</a> death in 1482 the realm was divided among his sons, producing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bhaktapur">Kingdom of Bhaktapur</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kantipur">Kingdom of Kantipur</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lalitpur">Kingdom of Lalitpur</a>. These Malla states lasted until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha</a> conquest of 1768 to 1769.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/malla-dynasty-nepal.jpg" alt="Malla dynasty (Nepal)" width="320" height="309"><figcaption>Image: Zulufive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nepaul_valley_map_1802.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1201 CE – 1482 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bhaktapur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ari Malla, Jayasthiti Malla, Yaksha Malla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Newar Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Newari (Nepal Bhasa), Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malla kingdom of Nepal, Nepal Mandala (Malla)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1342&amp;focus=malla-dynasty-nepal" aria-label="Malla dynasty (Nepal) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6922 1163 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Malla dynasty (Nepal) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7242 1419L7239 1418M7242 1419L7242 1419M7242 1419L7239 1418"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7239 1418h0M7242 1419h0M7239 1418h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7242 1419h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7242 1419h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bhaktapur. <a href="/atlas?year=1342&amp;focus=malla-dynasty-nepal">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
